{
  "chapter": "security/identity",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "The common defect: the agent authenticates the user and then calls every backend with its",
    "own service credential. Nothing is broken; everything is wrong.",
    "Three models. Service identity is the confused deputy; impersonation gets authorization",
    "right and destroys accountability; delegation names both principals \u2014 whose rights apply",
    "and who is exercising them.",
    "Downscope on the way in. Filtering results after a privileged read is a display",
    "preference: the content was still read, still logged, still seen by the model, and the",
    "backend's own audit log now disagrees with your policy.",
    "Agents break the request-scoped assumption, so store the delegation reference in the run",
    "state and never the token. A resumed run replaying Monday's rights is a revocation that",
    "silently did not happen.",
    "A scheduled agent with no user still needs a named human owner and an expiry that forces",
    "re-approval.",
    "Every privileged action names the user, the agent and the run. Any two of the three leave",
    "'why was this allowed' unanswerable."
  ],
  "cases": [
